\begin{abstract}
The pigeonhole principle forbids a code that decodes every source word exactly with fewer codewords than source words. We study the \emph{almost-lossless} relaxation, in which the decoder may fail with probability at most $\varepsilon$ but never \emph{silently}, and determine precisely how much the counting bound relaxes.

On the rate side we prove an exact characterisation --- the \emph{$\varepsilon$-relaxed pigeonhole principle} --- stating that a codeword alphabet $C$ admits an $\varepsilon$-reliable code for a source $\mu$ if and only if some set of at most $|C|$ source words carries probability at least $1-\varepsilon$. For a uniform source this specialises to $|C| \ge (1-\varepsilon)|S|$, and this bound is unaffected by shared randomness: every randomized ensemble has average failure probability at least $1 - |C|/|S|$, and for every source an ensemble with average failure $\le \varepsilon$ yields a deterministic code with failure $\le \varepsilon$. Honesty (no silent corruption) is free: any code is matched, on its correct set, by an honest uniqueness-scan code whose decoder probes at most one candidate.

On the construction side we give a Monte Carlo scheme based on $2$-universal hashing with average failure probability at most $\varepsilon + t(t-1)/|M|$ for a typical set of size $t$, honest for \emph{every} seed, with an exactly computed decoding cost. Derandomising by the probabilistic method and Bertrand's postulate yields an explicit deterministic scheme over $\F_p$ with $t^2 < p \le 2t^2$ decoding every typical word correctly with $p+1 \le 2t^2+1$ symbols: a quadratic rate penalty relative to the optimum $t$, and nothing more.

Centrally, we treat decoder complexity. For a bucketed two-hash decoder with a pairwise independent bucket hash of range $m_1$, the expected number of candidate tests for a typical word is \emph{exactly} $1 + (t-1)/m_1$. Against this we prove a universal converse by Cauchy--Schwarz over codeword fibres: for every candidate-scanning scheme and every seed, the total decoding work over the typical set is at least $t^2/|M|$, so the average per-word cost is at least $t/|M|$. Rate and decoding time obey a hyperbolic trade-off, and the bucketed decoder is optimal to within an additive $1$. Finally, in the plane the Monte Carlo failure probability is computed exactly: for $T \subseteq \F_p^2$ whose difference set realises $d$ distinct projective directions, exactly $1 + d(p-1)$ seeds are bad, giving failure probability exactly $(1+d(p-1))/p^2$; a worked example over $\F_{11}$ shows the union bound loose by a factor $2.13$.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The exact bound and the crack in it}

Let $S$ be a finite set of source words and $C$ a finite set of codewords. A \emph{lossless} fixed-length code is a pair $(\enc,\dec)$ with $\dec(\enc(s)) = s$ for all $s$; the pigeonhole principle forces $|C| \ge |S|$. This is why there is no universal compressor, and it is entirely insensitive to the cleverness of the encoder.

The relaxation studied here is the one in which practical information theory lives: the decoder may fail on a set of source words of probability at most $\varepsilon$. The question is threefold. How far does the counting bound relax? Can shared randomness (a random number generator) help? What decoding complexity results? We answer all three, and impose a fourth requirement that we regard as non-negotiable: \textbf{no silent corruption}. Every scheme below is \emph{honest} --- on every input it returns the true source word or an explicit failure symbol.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Exact relaxed pigeonhole (Theorem~\ref{thm:eps-pigeon}): $\varepsilon$-reliability with alphabet $C$ iff a set of size $\le |C|$ carries probability $\ge 1-\varepsilon$.
\item Randomness is worthless for rate (Theorems~\ref{thm:rand-conv}, \ref{thm:derand-general}).
\item Honesty is free (Theorem~\ref{thm:honesty-free}).
\item Monte Carlo scheme with detected failure (Theorem~\ref{thm:mc}).
\item Explicit derandomised scheme at quadratic rate (Theorem~\ref{thm:quad}).
\item Exact expected decoder cost $1 + (t-1)/m_1$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:exact-cost}).
\item Universal rate--time converse (Theorems~\ref{thm:cs}, \ref{thm:hyperbola}).
\item Exact planar failure probability $(1+d(p-1))/p^2$ (Theorems~\ref{thm:exact-bad}, \ref{thm:exact-prob}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Definitions}

All sets are finite, all probabilities are exact rationals, and every statement is one-shot; no asymptotics are used.

\begin{definition}[Code]
A \emph{code} from $S$ to $C$ is a pair $K = (\enc,\dec)$ with $\enc : S \to C$ and $\dec : C \to S \cup \{\bot\}$, where $\bot$ is an explicit decoding failure.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Correctness]
The word $s$ is \emph{decoded correctly}, written $\Corr_K(s)$, if $\dec(\enc(s)) = s$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Honesty]
$K$ is \emph{honest} if for every $s$, either $\dec(\enc(s)) = s$ or $\dec(\enc(s)) = \bot$; equivalently the decoder never returns a wrong word.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Source, failure probability]
A \emph{source} is $\mu : S \to \mathbb{Q}_{\ge 0}$ with $\sum_s \mu(s) = 1$; $\mu(A) = \sum_{s \in A}\mu(s)$. The \emph{uniform source} has $\mu(s) = 1/|S|$. The \emph{failure probability} is $\Pfail(\mu,K) = \mu(\{s : \neg\Corr_K(s)\})$, and $K$ is \emph{$\varepsilon$-reliable} if $\Pfail \le \varepsilon$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Randomized ensemble]
For a finite nonempty seed set $\Omega$, an ensemble is $(K_\omega)_{\omega\in\Omega}$, with average failure probability under a uniform seed
$\Pfailbar(\mu,K) = |\Omega|^{-1}\sum_{\omega} \Pfail(\mu, K_\omega)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Hash families]
A \emph{hash family} is $h : A \times S \to M$, written $h_a(x)$, with $A$ (seeds) and $M$ (range) finite. It is \emph{$2$-universal} if for all $x \ne y$,
$\#\{a : h_a(x) = h_a(y)\}\cdot|M| \le |A|$, and \emph{pairwise independent} if equality always holds. A seed $a$ \emph{collides on} $T \subseteq S$ if $h_a$ is not injective on $T$; such seeds are called \emph{bad}.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Scan scheme]\label{def:scan}
A \emph{scan scheme} consists of a \emph{typical set} $T \subseteq S$, a hash family $h : A \times S \to M$ used as the codeword, and candidate sets $\cand_a(m) \subseteq T$ satisfying $x \in T \implies x \in \cand_a(h_a(x))$ (the true word is always a candidate).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Uniqueness-scan decoder]
On receiving $m$, the decoder tests each $y \in \cand_a(m)$ for $h_a(y) = m$ and returns $y$ if \emph{exactly one} candidate matches, and $\bot$ otherwise. Its \emph{cost} is $\cost_a(m) = |\cand_a(m)|$. The induced code sends $h_a(s)$ for $s \in T$ and $\bot$ otherwise, so its alphabet has $|M|+1$ symbols.
\end{definition}

Two instances recur: the \emph{linear scan}, $\cand_a(m) = T$, of cost exactly $|T|$; and the \emph{bucketed scheme}, with independently seeded hashes $h_1 : A_1\times S \to M_1$ and $h_2 : A_2 \times S \to M_2$, codeword $(h_1(x),h_2(x))$ and $\cand_{(a_1,a_2)}(m) = \{y \in T : h_{1,a_1}(y) = m_1\}$.

\section{The rate side}

\begin{theorem}[Counting Lemma]\label{thm:count}
For every code $K : S \to C$ the set $\{s : \Corr_K(s)\}$ injects into $C$; hence it has at most $|C|$ elements, and at least $|S|-|C|$ source words fail to decode.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $\Corr_K(x)$, $\Corr_K(y)$ and $\enc(x) = \enc(y)$ then $x = \dec(\enc(x)) = \dec(\enc(y)) = y$, so $\enc$ is injective on the correct set.
\end{proof}

Neither honesty nor probability enters: this is the exact residue of the pigeonhole principle.

\begin{theorem}[Uniform converse]\label{thm:unif}
For the uniform source, every code $K : S \to C$ satisfies $\Pfail \ge 1 - |C|/|S|$; equivalently an $\varepsilon$-reliable code obeys $(1-\varepsilon)|S| \le |C|$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The incorrect set has at least $|S|-|C|$ elements by Theorem~\ref{thm:count}, each of weight $1/|S|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Randomness buys nothing]\label{thm:rand-conv}
For every finite nonempty $\Omega$ and every ensemble $(K_\omega)$ of codes into $C$, the average failure probability on the uniform source is at least $1 - |C|/|S|$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each summand is at least $b := 1-|C|/|S|$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:unif}, and a mean of numbers each $\ge b$ is $\ge b$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Generic achievability]\label{lem:ach}
If a code decodes every element of $T$ correctly and $\mu(T) \ge 1-\varepsilon$, then $\Pfail \le \varepsilon$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The incorrect set lies in $S\setminus T$, of probability $1-\mu(T) \le \varepsilon$.
\end{proof}

The prototypical achiever is the \emph{table code}: enumerate $T = \{t_0,\dots,t_{|T|-1}\}$, encode $t_i$ as $i$ and every atypical word as $\bot$, decode $i$ as $t_i$. It is honest, uses $|T|+1$ symbols, and decodes in one table lookup.

\begin{theorem}[$\varepsilon$-relaxed pigeonhole principle]\label{thm:eps-pigeon}
Let $\mu$ be a source on $S$ and $C$ a nonempty finite alphabet. Then there exists a code $K : S \to C$ with $\Pfail(\mu,K)\le\varepsilon$ if and only if there exists $T \subseteq S$ with $|T| \le |C|$ and $\mu(T) \ge 1-\varepsilon$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) Take $T = \{s : \Corr_K(s)\}$; then $|T| \le |C|$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:count} and $\mu(T) = 1-\Pfail \ge 1-\varepsilon$.
($\Leftarrow$) Choose an injection $f$ from an index set of $T$ into $C$, encode $t_i$ by $f(i)$ and atypical words by a fixed junk codeword, and decode $c$ to the unique preimage if one exists and $\bot$ otherwise. Lemma~\ref{lem:ach} applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The relaxation is not a multiplicative loosening of the counting bound: it converts ``how many codewords?'' into ``how small can a $(1-\varepsilon)$-mass set be?'', i.e.\ into the concentration function of the source. For a uniform source it collapses back to Theorem~\ref{thm:unif}; for a concentrated source the smallest such set may be exponentially smaller than $S$.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:mean}
For every source and ensemble, some seed satisfies $\Pfail(K_\omega) \le \Pfailbar$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[General derandomisation]\label{thm:derand-general}
For every source, if an ensemble into $C$ has $\Pfailbar \le \varepsilon$, then some set $T$ with $|T| \le |C|$ has $\mu(T)\ge 1-\varepsilon$, and hence some single deterministic code into $C$ has $\Pfail \le \varepsilon$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Combine Corollary~\ref{cor:mean} with both directions of Theorem~\ref{thm:eps-pigeon}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Honesty is free]\label{thm:honesty-free}
Let $K : S \to C$ be arbitrary. There is a scan scheme (trivial seed set) whose code $K'$ satisfies: $\Corr_{K'}(s) \iff \Corr_K(s)$ for all $s$; $K'$ is honest; and every decoding probes at most one candidate. The only cost is one extra alphabet symbol.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $T = \{s : \Corr_K(s)\}$, hash $h(s) = \enc(s)$, candidates $\cand(m) = \{s \in T : \enc(s) = m\}$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:count} the encoder is injective on $T$, so $|\cand(m)| \le 1$; the uniqueness-scan decoder then returns the true word on $T$ and $\bot$ elsewhere.
\end{proof}

\section{Hashing: the derandomised random codebook}

Shannon's argument draws a codebook uniformly at random; the only property ever used in almost-lossless source coding is that two fixed distinct words collide with probability at most $1/|M|$, i.e.\ $2$-universality. Replacing an exponentially large random codebook with a family whose seed is $k$ field elements is therefore lossless in the analysis and decisive in implementation.

\begin{theorem}[Union bound over pairs]\label{thm:union}
If $h$ is $2$-universal and $|T| = t$, then the number of bad seeds satisfies $\#\{a\ \text{bad}\}\cdot |M| \le t(t-1)\,|A|$; equivalently a uniform seed is bad with probability at most $t(t-1)/|M|$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The bad set is the union over ordered pairs of distinct $x,y \in T$ of $\{a : h_a(x) = h_a(y)\}$, each of size $\le |A|/|M|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Probabilistic method]\label{thm:perfect}
If $|M| > t(t-1)$, some seed is injective on $T$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Inner-product family]\label{thm:dot}
Let $p$ be prime and $h_a(x) = \langle a,x\rangle$ on $\F_p^k$. For all $x \ne y$, exactly a $1/p$ fraction of seeds collide; in particular the family is pairwise independent, hence $2$-universal.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
With $z = x-y \ne 0$, the map $a \mapsto \langle a,z\rangle$ is a nonzero additive homomorphism $\F_p^k \to \F_p$, hence surjective, and all fibres of a surjective homomorphism of finite abelian groups have equal size $p^{k-1}$.
\end{proof}

Two independently seeded $2$-universal families combine coordinatewise into a $2$-universal family into $M_1\times M_2$; and $2$-universality is preserved by precomposition with an injection, which allows an arbitrary finite source to be embedded in a vector space and hashed.

\section{The Monte Carlo scheme}

\begin{theorem}[Unconditional honesty]\label{thm:honest}
For every seed --- including seeds for which $h_a$ collides all over $T$ --- the code induced by a scan scheme is honest.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A non-$\bot$ answer is returned only when exactly one candidate matches. If $x \in T$ was transmitted, $x$ is itself a matching candidate, so uniqueness forces the answer to be $x$. An atypical word is encoded as $\bot$ and decoded as $\bot$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact decoding cost]\label{thm:cost}
Decoding $m$ under seed $a$ performs exactly $|\cand_a(m)|$ hash evaluations and comparisons, in a single pass with no search or backtracking. In particular the linear-scan decoder costs exactly $|T|$ tests.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Correctness under a good seed]\label{thm:good-seed}
If $h_a$ is injective on $T$, every typical word decodes correctly.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Monte Carlo reliability]\label{thm:mc}
Let $h$ be $2$-universal, $\mu(T) \ge 1-\varepsilon$ with $\varepsilon\ge 0$, $t = |T|$, and draw the seed uniformly. Then
\[
\Pfailbar \;\le\; \varepsilon + \frac{t(t-1)}{|M|},
\]
and every failure is detected: the code is honest for every seed.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Split the seeds into the bad set $B$ and its complement. For $a \notin B$, Theorem~\ref{thm:good-seed} and Lemma~\ref{lem:ach} give $\Pfail(K_a) \le \varepsilon$; for $a \in B$ use $\Pfail \le 1$. Averaging, $\Pfailbar \le \varepsilon + |B|/|A| \le \varepsilon + t(t-1)/|M|$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:union}. Honesty is Theorem~\ref{thm:honest}.
\end{proof}

The two error terms differ in character: $\varepsilon$ is \emph{atypicality loss}, irreducible by Theorem~\ref{thm:eps-pigeon}; $t(t-1)/|M|$ is \emph{collision loss}, an artefact of the construction, removable by enlarging $M$ or by derandomising.

\begin{example}[A concrete compressor]
Take $S = \F_p^k$, seed $a \in \F_p^k$ from a random number generator shared by encoder and decoder, codeword $\langle a,x\rangle \in \F_p$ plus a failure flag: $p+1$ symbols against $p^k$ source words. The scheme is honest for every seed, decodes in exactly $|T|$ tests, and fails with average probability at most $\varepsilon + t(t-1)/p$.
\end{example}

\begin{remark}[No paradox]
On the \emph{uniform} source over $\F_p^k$, the same $(p+1)$-symbol alphabet fails with probability at least $1 - (p+1)/p^k$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:unif}. The compressor wins only because the source is concentrated; the randomness of the seed contributes nothing to rate.
\end{remark}

\section{Derandomisation and the price of explicitness}

\begin{theorem}[Perfect seed]\label{thm:perfect-scheme}
If $h$ is $2$-universal and $|M| > t(t-1)$, some seed makes the linear-scan code honest, correct on every typical word (zero failure on $T$), with cost exactly $t$; hence a deterministic code with $\Pfail \le \varepsilon$ exists whenever $\mu(T) \ge 1-\varepsilon$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:embed}
Any finite $S$ embeds into $\F_p^{|S|}$ for $p \ge 2$, since $|S| \le 2^{|S|} \le p^{|S|}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Quadratic-rate deterministic scheme]\label{thm:quad}
For every nonempty typical set $T$ of size $t$ there exist a prime $p$ with $t^2 < p \le 2t^2$ and an explicit inner-product hash scheme over $\F_p$, with an explicit seed, whose code is honest, decodes every word of $T$ correctly, costs exactly $t$ candidate tests per decoding, and transmits one of only $p+1 \le 2t^2+1$ symbols.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Bertrand's postulate gives a prime $p \in (t^2, 2t^2]$. Embed $S$ into $\F_p^{|S|}$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:embed}) and use the inner-product family, $2$-universal by Theorem~\ref{thm:dot}. Since the number of ordered pairs of distinct typical words is $t^2 - t < p$, Theorem~\ref{thm:perfect-scheme} applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Birthday penalty]
Theorem~\ref{thm:eps-pigeon} says $t$ symbols suffice information-theoretically, via a table; Theorem~\ref{thm:quad} achieves $\Theta(t^2)$ with no table at all. In bits, the constructive scheme spends $2\log_2 t + O(1)$ where $\log_2 t$ would do --- a factor-two rate loss in exchange for a compact, evaluable description.
\end{remark}

\section{Decoder complexity}

\subsection{An exact expected cost}

For the bucketed scheme write $m_1 = |M_1|$ and
$\col(a_1,x) = \#\{y \in T\setminus\{x\} : h_{1,a_1}(y) = h_{1,a_1}(x)\}$
for the number of false candidates.

\begin{lemma}[Bucket size]\label{lem:bucket}
For $x \in T$ the bucket containing $x$ has size $1 + \col(a_1,x)$, and this is exactly the decoder's cost when $x$ is transmitted.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Expected false candidates]\label{lem:falsecand}
If $h_1$ is pairwise independent then for every $x$,
\[
\frac{1}{|A_1|}\sum_{a_1 \in A_1}\col(a_1,x) \;=\; \frac{|T\setminus\{x\}|}{m_1},
\]
with $\le$ in place of $=$ if $h_1$ is merely $2$-universal.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Exchange summation: $\sum_{a_1}\col(a_1,x) = \sum_{y \in T\setminus\{x\}} \#\{a_1 : h_{1,a_1}(y)=h_{1,a_1}(x)\} = |T\setminus\{x\}|\cdot|A_1|/m_1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact expected decoder cost]\label{thm:exact-cost}
Let $h_1$ be pairwise independent with $m_1$ buckets, $x \in T$, $t = |T|$. Then, averaged over a uniform bucket-hash seed, the expected number of candidate tests performed when $x$ is transmitted is exactly
\[
\mathbb{E}[\cost] \;=\; 1 + \frac{t-1}{m_1}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Average Lemma~\ref{lem:bucket} over $a_1$ and insert Lemma~\ref{lem:falsecand}.
\end{proof}

Thus for $m_1 \ge t$ the expected cost is below $2$ --- constant-time decoding, against $t$ for the naive scan. Because Theorem~\ref{thm:exact-cost} is an identity, the corresponding upper bound cannot be improved for pairwise independent families. The checksum hash $h_2$ does not enter the cost at all: its role is to keep the failure probability small while the bucket hash keeps the work small, so rate and time are controlled by two independent knobs.

\subsection{A universal converse}

\begin{theorem}[Total-work lower bound]\label{thm:cs}
For every scan scheme with typical set $T$, $|T| = t$, codeword set $M$, and every seed $a$,
\[
\sum_{x \in T} \cost_a(h_a(x)) \;\ge\; \frac{t^2}{|M|}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Partition $T$ into fibres $F_m = \{y \in T : h_a(y) = m\}$ of sizes $n_m$ with $\sum_m n_m = t$. If $y \in F_{h_a(x)}$ then $y \in \cand_a(h_a(y)) = \cand_a(h_a(x))$ by Definition~\ref{def:scan}, so $F_{h_a(x)} \subseteq \cand_a(h_a(x))$ and $\cost_a(h_a(x)) \ge n_{h_a(x)}$. Summing over $x \in T$ and grouping by fibre,
\[
\sum_{x\in T}\cost_a(h_a(x)) \;\ge\; \sum_{m\in M} n_m^2 \;\ge\; \frac{1}{|M|}\Big(\sum_m n_m\Big)^2 = \frac{t^2}{|M|},
\]
the last step by Cauchy--Schwarz.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Rate--time hyperbola]\label{thm:hyperbola}
For every scan scheme with nonempty typical set and every seed, the average cost of decoding a typical word is at least $t/|M|$; moreover every decoding costs at least one test.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
Writing $m = |M|$, Theorems~\ref{thm:exact-cost} and \ref{thm:hyperbola} read
\[
\max\Big(1,\tfrac{t}{m}\Big) \;\le\; \mathbb{E}[\cost] \;\le\; 1 + \frac{t-1}{m},
\]
a gap of less than one probe uniformly in $t$ and $m$. Rate and time are conjugate: compressing harder forces the decoder to sift a proportionally larger bucket. The table code escapes only because it uses $m = t$ codewords, where the hyperbola bottoms out at $1$.
\end{remark}

\section{Exact failure probability: from probability to projective geometry}

Let $p$ be prime, $S = \F_p^2$, $h_a(x) = \langle a,x\rangle$, and for $z \ne 0$ let $z^\perp = \{a : \langle a,z\rangle = 0\}$, a line through the origin with exactly $p$ points. A seed is bad for $T$ iff it lies in $z^\perp$ for some difference $z = x - y$ of distinct typical words; since $(\lambda z)^\perp = z^\perp$ for $\lambda\ne0$, only the \emph{projective} direction of $z$ matters.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:meet}
If $z,w$ are nonzero with $z_0w_1 - z_1w_0 \ne 0$ then $z^\perp \cap w^\perp = \{0\}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Pencil count]\label{thm:pencil}
If $D$ is a nonempty set of pairwise non-proportional nonzero directions with $|D| = d$, then $\#\bigcup_{z\in D} z^\perp = 1 + d(p-1)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each $z^\perp$ has the origin and $p-1$ further points, and by Lemma~\ref{lem:meet} the punctured lines are pairwise disjoint.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact bad-seed count]\label{thm:exact-bad}
Let $T \subseteq \F_p^2$ and let $D$ be a set of representatives of the projective directions realised by the differences of $T$: nonzero, pairwise non-proportional, covering every difference of distinct elements of $T$, and each occurring as such a difference. Then the number of seeds colliding on $T$ is exactly $1 + |D|(p-1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Exact planar failure probability]\label{thm:exact-prob}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:exact-bad} with $d = |D|$, a uniformly random seed is bad with probability exactly $(1 + d(p-1))/p^2$. Since $d \le t(t-1)/2$, this never exceeds the union bound $t(t-1)/p$ materially, and is strictly smaller as soon as two differences are proportional.
\end{theorem}

\begin{example}
Let $p = 11$ and $T = \{(1,0),(0,1),(2,3)\} \subseteq \F_{11}^2$. Its three differences $(1,10)$, $(10,8)$, $(9,9)$ are pairwise non-proportional, so $d = 3$ and the count is $1 + 3\cdot 10 = 31$ bad seeds among $121$: failure probability exactly $31/121 \approx 0.2562$. Exhaustive enumeration of all $121$ seeds confirms $31$. The union bound gives $6/11 = 66/121 \approx 0.5455$, loose by a factor $2.13$.
\end{example}

\begin{remark}
``Probability that a random seed collides'' has become ``normalised measure of a pencil of hyperplanes''. Lower bounds on the number $d$ of distinct projective directions of a difference set --- a purely geometric quantity --- become lower bounds on the failure probability, and hence on the range $|M|$ any pairwise independent family must use.
\end{remark}

\section{Discussion and future work}

\subsection{Synthesis}
The pigeonhole bound relaxes exactly to the concentration function of the source (Theorem~\ref{thm:eps-pigeon}); it does not relax at all for a uniform source (Theorem~\ref{thm:unif}); shared randomness cannot change this for any source (Theorems~\ref{thm:rand-conv}, \ref{thm:derand-general}). Hashing recovers near-optimality without tables at a quadratic rate penalty, with unconditional honesty (Theorems~\ref{thm:mc}, \ref{thm:quad}). Rate and decoding time obey a hyperbola achieved to within an additive $1$ by bucketing (Theorems~\ref{thm:exact-cost}, \ref{thm:hyperbola}). In the plane the failure probability is not merely bounded but computed (Theorem~\ref{thm:exact-prob}).

Every scheme meets the falsifiability gate --- a failure bound plus an explicit complexity figure, with no silent corruption: the table code ($\Pfail\le\varepsilon$, cost $1$, alphabet $t+1$); the linear-scan Monte Carlo scheme ($\Pfail \le \varepsilon + t(t-1)/|M|$, cost exactly $t$, alphabet $|M|+1$); the derandomised inner-product scheme ($\Pfail \le \varepsilon$ deterministically, cost exactly $t$, alphabet $\le 2t^2+1$); the bucketed scheme (expected cost exactly $1 + (t-1)/m_1$).

\subsection{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Birthday penalty is unavoidable for pairwise independent codebooks]
Let $h$ be pairwise independent (an equality, not merely the $2$-universal inequality). For every $T$ with $|T| \le \sqrt{|M|}$ whose difference set has $\Omega(|T|^2)$ distinct projective directions, the probability that a uniform seed is injective on $T$ satisfies $P(\text{injective}) \le \exp(-c|T|^2/|M|)$. Consequently no pairwise independent family attains the information-theoretic alphabet size $|T|$, and the squaring in Theorem~\ref{thm:quad} is necessary in this class.
\end{conjecture}
The key insight is that the exact planar count turns ``probability of a collision'' into ``measure of a pencil of hyperplanes'', so lower bounds on the number of projective directions become lower bounds on the failure probability. The planar case is an equality already; the missing ingredient is inclusion--exclusion over the subspace lattice in dimension $k \ge 3$.

\begin{conjecture}[Exact failure probability in all dimensions]
For the inner-product family over $\F_p^k$ and a typical set whose difference directions are $z_1,\dots,z_d$, the number of bad seeds equals
\[
\sum_{\emptyset \ne I \subseteq [d]} (-1)^{|I|+1}\, p^{\,k-\dim\spn\{z_i : i \in I\}},
\]
i.e.\ the M\"obius function of the lattice of subspaces generated by the directions governs the exact failure probability.
\end{conjecture}
The bad set is a union of hyperplanes, so the exact count is a matroid/M\"obius computation over $\F_p$, not a probabilistic estimate; the union bound is precisely the first Bonferroni term. The $k=2$ instance is proved (pairwise intersections trivial, so the expansion truncates), and the failure of the naive formula at $p=13$, $k=3$ isolates the missing higher-order terms.

\begin{conjecture}[Constant-time decoding at optimal rate]
There is an explicit scheme with codeword alphabet of size $(1+o(1))|T|$ --- matching Theorem~\ref{thm:eps-pigeon}, with no squaring --- whose decoder performs $O(1)$ expected candidate tests, obtained by composing a bucket hash of range $|T|$ with a rate-optimal index inside each bucket.
\end{conjecture}
The rate--time hyperbola permits this: at $m = t$ the lower bound on expected cost is exactly $1$. The obstruction is constructive, not information-theoretic.

Further threads: affine families $h_a(x) = \langle a,x\rangle + b$, where the bad set is a union of affine hyperplanes and the count acquires a geometric-lattice flavour; a bit-level statement of the hyperbola, $\log_2 m + \log_2 \mathbb{E}[\cost] \ge \log_2 t$, as a conservation law between transmitted bits and search bits; multi-shot and streaming variants with factored typical sets; and adversarial seeds, where the average over seeds is replaced by a worst-case guarantee under a computational assumption, with unconditional honesty retained as the safety net.

\section{Conclusion}

Relaxing exact decoding to $\varepsilon$-reliable decoding does not weaken the pigeonhole bound so much as change its subject: from the cardinality of the source alphabet to the concentration of the source distribution. Randomness contributes nothing to rate in any setting, serving only to avoid an explicit search. What it does not eliminate is time: once fast decoding is required, a second conservation law appears, bounding rate against expected decoding work along a hyperbola that a two-hash bucketed decoder attains to within a single probe --- while announcing every one of its failures.

